NAME
       date - print or set the system date and time

SYNOPSIS
       date [-u] [-d TEXT] [+FORMAT]
       date -s TEXT
       date -I | -R

DESCRIPTION
       date prints the current date and time in the local time zone:

           Mon Mar  2 04:00:00 AM EST 2026

       With +FORMAT it prints exactly the format you give, which is how
       scripts make time stamps and dated file names. With -d it shows
       another date instead of now. Only root can set the clock with -s; on
       a machine that uses NTP (chronyd), the clock is normally left to it.

       The time zone comes from /etc/localtime; change it with timedatectl
       set-timezone.

   Format codes
       %Y year (2026)          %m month (01-12)       %d day (01-31)
       %H hour (00-23)         %M minute              %S second
       %F same as %Y-%m-%d     %T same as %H:%M:%S    %D same as %m/%d/%y
       %a weekday (Mon)        %A weekday (Monday)    %b month (Mar)
       %B month (March)        %e day, space-padded   %j day of the year
       %u weekday, 1 = Monday  %w weekday, 0 = Sunday %s seconds since 1970
       %Z time zone (EST)      %p AM or PM            %I hour (01-12)
       %y year (26)            %R same as %H:%M       %c date and time
       %% a literal %

OPTIONS
       -d TEXT, --date=TEXT
              Show TEXT instead of now. TEXT may be a date (2026-03-05), a
              date and time, or words such as tomorrow, yesterday, "next
              monday", "3 days ago", "+2 weeks", or @SECONDS.

       -s TEXT, --set=TEXT
              Set the clock to TEXT (root only).

       -u, --utc, --universal
              Use UTC instead of the local time zone.

       -I, --iso-8601
              Print the date as YYYY-MM-DD.

       -R, --rfc-email
              Print the date as in e-mail headers.

EXAMPLES
       A time stamp for a file name:

           cp /etc/hosts /root/hosts-$(date +%F).bak

       The weekday of a date:

           date -d 2026-12-25 +%A

       The time in UTC:

           date -u

       In a crontab line, write % as \%:

           0 1 * * * tar -czf /root/etc-$(date +\%F).tar.gz /etc

SEE ALSO
       timedatectl(1), chronyc(1), hwclock(8), at(1)
